    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Quickstart\"\nguide-section: \"Getting Started\"\n\nbread-crumbs: false\nexecute:\n  freeze: true\n---\n\n# Quickstart\n\nThis example processes two acquisitions of one burst over the Aletsch glacier\nin the Swiss Alps. It produces terrain-corrected backscatter for each date and\nthe coherence between them. Complete [Installation](installation.qmd) first, and\nrun the script with your\n[credentials in the environment](environment-variables.qmd). The example\ndownloads about 200 MB and runs SNAP.\n\n\n## Find two acquisitions\n\nA search returns the catalog records that match a query. This one covers two\nweeks of one burst footprint, which Sentinel-1 imaged twice, 12 days apart:\n\n::: {#b5634ce9 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfrom s1bursts import BurstCatalog, BurstProcessor, FileManager, SearchQuery, TimeRange\n\nfm = FileManager(\"data/quickstart\")\ncatalog = BurstCatalog(fm)\n\nbursts = catalog.search(SearchQuery(\n    time_range=TimeRange(\"2022-08-01\", \"2022-08-13\"),\n    burst_id=30346, sub_swath=\"IW1\", polarization=\"VV\",\n))\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Download and process\n\nThe download stores both acquisitions under `data/quickstart/s1-slc`.\nProcessing then writes one GeoTIFF per product under `data/quickstart/s1-prod`.\nWith `crs=\"auto\"`, each burst is projected in the UTM zone that holds it, here\nzone 32 North (EPSG:32632). Backscatter is processed per burst. Coherence needs\na pair of acquisitions, which `find_pairs()` builds from the same bursts:\n\n::: {#b2c3d1bc .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncatalog.download(bursts).raise_on_failure()\n\nprocessor = BurstProcessor(fm)\nrtc = processor.create_backscatter(bursts, crs=\"auto\").raise_on_failure()\n\npairs = bursts.find_pairs(baseline=12)\ncoherence = processor.create_coherences(pairs, crs=\"auto\").raise_on_failure()\n```\n:::\n\n\nA burst that fails does not stop the others. `raise_on_failure()` raises an\nerror naming every failed burst, and otherwise returns the summary of the\nrun. Running the script again skips every file that already exists, after\nchecking that it was made with the same settings.\n\n## Look at the result\n\nEach product is a GeoTIFF that any GIS or raster library can open, and\n`fm.open()` opens one with rasterio. This plots the backscatter of the first\ndate in decibels, with the coherence below it:\n\n::: {#ba94038d .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport numpy as np\n\nwith fm.open(rtc.products[0]) as src:\n    gamma0 = src.read(1, masked=True)\nwith fm.open(coherence.products[0]) as src:\n    coh = src.read(1, masked=True)\n\nfig, (top, bottom) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(7.8, 4.7), layout=\"constrained\")\nfig.colorbar(top.imshow(10 * np.log10(gamma0), cmap=\"viridis\", vmin=-20, vmax=0),\n             ax=top, label=\"Backscatter (dB)\")\nfig.colorbar(bottom.imshow(coh, cmap=\"magma\", vmin=0, vmax=1), ax=bottom, label=\"Coherence\")\nfor ax in (top, bottom):\n    ax.set_axis_off()\nplt.show()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![](quickstart_files/figure-html/cell-5-output-1.png){width=632 height=462}\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe burst covers a strip of the Alps about 85 km long. Coherence is high\n(light) where the ground changed little in the 12 days, such as bare rock and\nmeadows, and low (dark) where it changed, for example on the melting\nglaciers and in forests.\n\n## What you get\n\nThe processing calls return two kinds of GeoTIFF:\n\n- **Backscatter** (one file per date): how strongly the ground reflects the\n  radar signal, corrected for terrain.\n- **Coherence** (one file): how similar the two acquisitions are, from 0 (no\n  similarity) to 1.\n\n[Products and bands](products.qmd) explains each band and its units. The\n[glacier tutorials](glacier-backscatter.qmd) take the same burst further.\n\n::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n## Run details\n\nRun on 09 November 2026 with s1bursts 0.9.0 and ESA SNAP 13.0.0. Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data (2022).\n:::\n\n\n",
